<h2>Timing the ask</h2>

<p>When you ask for a referral matters just as much as what you offer. Ask too early, before the customer has experienced the full value of your service, and they do not have enough conviction to recommend you. Ask too late, and the emotional peak has passed.</p>

<p>There are three golden windows for the referral ask.</p>

<p><strong>Right after a great experience.</strong> This is the most powerful moment. The customer just had an excellent interaction with your business. They are feeling positive, grateful, maybe even a little surprised by how good the experience was. This is when they are most likely to say yes to anything you ask. A well-timed message within 24 hours of a completed service can capture that energy. If you are already <a href="/blog/get-more-google-reviews">asking for a Google review</a> at this point, you can bundle the referral ask alongside it or follow up with it a day later.</p>

<p><strong>After a positive review.</strong> When someone takes the time to leave you a five-star review, they have already publicly declared that they love your business. That is a signal. Follow up with a thank-you message and a soft referral ask: "Thanks so much for the kind words. If you know anyone who could use the same service, we would love to take care of them. Here is a link to share." The person who just wrote you a glowing review is primed to refer.</p>

<p><strong>After a loyalty milestone.</strong> Has a customer been with you for a year? Completed their tenth appointment? Crossed a spending threshold? Milestones create natural moments of reflection. "You have been with us for a year now, and we really appreciate your loyalty. As a thank you, here is a special referral offer for you and anyone you would like to share it with." It feels personal, earned, and well-timed.</p>

<blockquote><p>The best time to ask for a referral is when your customer is already thinking about how great you are. Your job is to be present in that moment.</p></blockquote>

