<h2>Automating the referral ask</h2>

<p>Knowing when to ask is one thing. Actually asking, consistently, for every customer, at the right moment, is another. That is where automation comes in.</p>

<p>The goal is to set up trigger-based workflows that send the referral ask automatically when the conditions are right. You build it once and it runs forever. Here are the key automations to put in place.</p>

<p><strong>Post-service trigger.</strong> When an appointment is marked complete or a project is delivered, the system waits 24 hours and then sends a referral message. This could be an <a href="/blog/sms-marketing-small-business">SMS</a> with a personal touch: "Hey Sarah, glad we could help. If you know anyone who might need the same thing, here is a link to share. You will both get $25 off." Short, friendly, one-tap easy.</p>

<p><strong>Post-review trigger.</strong> When a customer submits a positive review (4 or 5 stars), the system automatically sends a follow-up referral message the next day. You already know they are happy. You are simply making it easy for them to spread the word.</p>

<p><strong>Loyalty milestone trigger.</strong> Set up rules based on customer activity: number of bookings, months since first purchase, or total spend. When they cross a threshold, the referral offer lands in their inbox or on their phone.</p>

<p>For the messages themselves, keep them short and make the action as simple as possible. A single link that the customer can forward to a friend, or share in a text message, is ideal. The link should pre-populate the referrer's information so you know where the lead came from. No forms to fill out, no codes to remember.</p>

<p>Here is a template you can use for SMS:</p>

<p><em>"Hey [Name], thanks for choosing us. If you know someone who could use our help, share this link and you will both get [incentive]: [referral link]"</em></p>

<p>And for <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email</a>, you have room to add a bit more context. Include a brief reminder of what you do, a clear explanation of the reward, and a prominent button or link. Keep the design clean and the copy conversational.</p>

<p>The key principle: if a customer has to think about how to refer, you have made it too hard. One tap, one link, done.</p>

